<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhenaten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1353–1336 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhenaten was an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> who reigned c. 1353–1336 BCE. He promoted the exclusive worship of the sun disc, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, and moved the capital to a new city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna). His religious revolution disrupted Egyptian tradition and was reversed after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhenaten.jpg" alt="Akhenaten" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GD-EG-Caire-Mus%C3%A9e061.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Akhenaten was born Amenhotep IV, a younger son of the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and his principal wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>. His elder brother, the crown prince Thutmose, was recognised as heir, but Thutmose's early death, perhaps around the 30th regnal year of Amenhotep III, left Amenhotep IV next in line. Little is known of his youth. Donald Redford dates his birth to c. 1363–1361 BCE, and the only certain servant of the prince is Parennefer, whose tomb mentions him.</p><p>He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>, his Great Royal Wife, at a date that is not known. A secondary wife, Kiya, is also attested. He had seven attested daughters, six of them by Nefertiti, among them Meritaten and Ankhesenamun. Tutankhamun was his son-in-law and is considered likely to have been his son as well.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Amenhotep IV took the throne most likely in 1353 or 1351 BCE, at an age estimated anywhere from 10 to 23. Scholars disagree whether he shared power with his father for up to 12 years or not at all. Eric Cline, Nicholas Reeves and Peter Dorman argue for no coregency or one of at most two years, while Donald Redford and others reject any. In the fifth year of his reign he changed his name to Akhenaten, meaning 'Effective for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>'.</p><p>Worship of the sun had grown under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> had given the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> royal patronage, but he still devoted himself chiefly to Amun-Ra. Redford traced three stages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>. In the first the disc still rested on the head of the falcon-headed Ra-Horakhty. In the second the Aten was raised above other gods and its name was placed in cartouches. In the last it was shown as a disc whose rays end in human hands. Egyptologists differ on whether the religion was monotheistic, monolatristic or henotheistic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> art, which flourished under Akhenaten and his immediate successors, is more naturalistic and shows more movement than traditional Egyptian art. Akhenaten himself is drawn with a sagging stomach, broad hips, thin legs and a long face. Some have argued that this reflects a medical condition, but Dominic Montserrat reports a broad consensus among Egyptologists that the forms are not to be read literally, and suggests they made the king a symbol of the androgyny of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>.</p><p>After his death his monuments were dismantled and hidden, his statues destroyed and his name left out of later lists of rulers, and traditional worship was gradually restored, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. Later rulers called him 'the enemy' or 'that criminal'. He was almost lost to history until the late 19th-century discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>, the city he built. A mummy found in tomb KV55 in 1907 may be his, although its identification has been questioned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akhenaten” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the fourteenth century BCE: King Rib-Hadda wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs Amenhotep III and <b>Akhenaten</b>, pleading for archers and supplies as Abdi-Ashirta of Amurru and then his son Aziru seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its own people against…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna Letters, the diplomatic archive recovered from <b>Akhenaten</b>'s capital, show an organised system of international correspondence conducted in Akkadian cuneiform, through which Egypt managed its relationships with Babylon, Mitanni, Assyria, the Hittites, and Alashiya (Cyprus).</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hymns to the gods were inscribed in temples and tombs; the Great Hymn to the Aten, from the time of <b>Akhenaten</b>, celebrates the sun as the creator of all life in terms often compared with Psalm 104 of the Hebrew Bible.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna letters, found in Egypt in 1887, contain the correspondence of the pharaohs Amenhotep III and <b>Akhenaten</b> with the kings of Babylon, Assyria, Mitanni and the Hittites and with the rulers of Canaan, almost all in Akkadian.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Old and Middle Egyptian were verb-first languages that expressed grammatical meanings with endings; Late Egyptian, which entered official writing under <b>Akhenaten</b> in the 14th century BCE, used articles, auxiliary verbs and a different word order; demotic and Coptic continued this development.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Their painted pottery, much of it containers for wine and perfumed oil, is found from Sardinia, Sicily and southern Italy to Cyprus, the Levant and Egypt, where it reached <b>Akhenaten</b>’s city at Amarna.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.72,32.61&amp;year=-1342" aria-label="Thebes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5413 1218 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Akhenaten"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5863 1488h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5863 1488h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.72,32.61&amp;year=-1342">What was here in 1342 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
